---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../../data/standards/ifrssustainability/ifrss1.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType="IFRS"
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Definitions" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Sustainability-related Risks and Opportunities" },
        { id: "s4", label: "The Four Content Pillars – TCFD Alignment" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Materiality Under IFRS S1" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Connected Information" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Sources of Guidance – SASB & Others" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Location and Timing of Reporting" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "IFRS S1 vs Other Frameworks (ESRS/GRI)" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Extending decision-usefulness beyond the financial statements</div>
        <div class="principle">The Conceptual Framework's core objective – providing information USEFUL to existing/potential investors, lenders, and creditors for resource allocation decisions – increasingly requires understanding risks and opportunities that traditional financial statements do NOT capture directly, particularly those arising from sustainability matters (climate, nature, social factors) affecting an entity's ENTERPRISE VALUE over the short, medium, and long term. IFRS S1 extends the Framework's decision-usefulness objective into this new disclosure domain.</div>
        <div class="elabel">HOW IFRS S1 MAPS TO THE FRAMEWORK</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span><strong>Relevance</strong> – sustainability-related risks/opportunities can materially affect an entity's future cash flows, and thus its enterprise value, even where not yet reflected in current financial statement figures</span>
          <span><strong>Faithful representation</strong> – IFRS S1 requires disclosure calibrated to genuine materiality, avoiding both omission of decision-useful information and "greenwashing" via unsubstantiated claims</span>
          <span><strong>Comparability</strong> – a single global baseline standard (rather than a patchwork of voluntary frameworks) aims to make sustainability disclosures genuinely comparable across entities and jurisdictions, mirroring IFRS's own historical rationale for global financial reporting convergence</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IFRS S1 was issued by the newly created International Sustainability Standards Board (ISSB), a SISTER board to the IASB under the same IFRS Foundation – reflecting the Foundation's expansion beyond financial reporting into sustainability disclosure as a formally connected but DISTINCT reporting domain.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">The "enterprise value" focus, at a glance</div>
        <div class="principle">IFRS S1 centres disclosure on information useful for assessing ENTERPRISE VALUE – not environmental/social impact for its own sake, but financially material effects on the reporting entity itself.</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-13-1"><span>Concept</span><span>IFRS S1's framing</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-13-1"><span>Focus</span><span>"Financial materiality" – effects ON the entity's value, cash flows, cost of capital</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-13-1"><span>Distinct from</span><span>"Double materiality" frameworks (e.g. EU ESRS) which ALSO require disclosure of the entity's impact ON society/environment</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout info callout--flush">This SINGLE (investor-focused) materiality lens is a KEY distinguishing feature of the ISSB's approach relative to broader multi-stakeholder sustainability reporting frameworks.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope & Definitions</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What IFRS S1 covers</div>
        <div class="principle">IFRS S1 requires an entity to disclose information about ALL sustainability-related risks and opportunities that could REASONABLY BE EXPECTED to affect the entity's cash flows, access to finance, or cost of capital over the short, medium, or long term – establishing the GENERAL, overarching disclosure framework that topic-specific standards (like IFRS S2) build upon.</div>
        <div class="note">IFRS S1 is designed to be APPLIED ALONGSIDE topic-specific standards such as IFRS S2 – it supplies the general architecture (materiality, content pillars, connected information) within which topic-specific disclosures are made.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions you must know</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Sustainability-related risks and opportunities</div><div class="glossary-def">Risks and opportunities that could reasonably be expected to affect an entity's enterprise value, arising from sustainability matters.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Enterprise value</div><div class="glossary-def">The value of an entity's business as a whole, encompassing the value attributable to equity and other capital providers – the central reference point for IFRS S1's materiality assessment.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">General purpose sustainability-related financial disclosures</div><div class="glossary-def">Disclosures designed to meet the common information needs of primary users, mirroring the terminology of general purpose financial statements.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Sustainability-related Risks and Opportunities</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">A time-horizon-explicit requirement</div>
        <div class="principle">An entity discloses information about sustainability-related risks/opportunities across THREE time horizons – SHORT, MEDIUM, and LONG term – as DEFINED by the entity itself, and CONSISTENT with the horizons used for related capital allocation and strategic planning decisions, and with horizons commonly used by the entity's INDUSTRY.</div>
        <div class="note">Requiring EXPLICIT time horizon disclosure directly serves the Framework's relevance objective – sustainability risks (e.g. climate physical risk) often materialise over MUCH longer horizons than traditional financial statement disclosures typically address, and IFRS S1 ensures this longer-term view is not lost.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>The Four Content Pillars – TCFD Alignment</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Governance, Strategy, Risk Management, Metrics and Targets</div>
        <div class="principle">IFRS S1 organises disclosure around FOUR core content areas, directly built on the widely adopted TCFD (Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures) recommendations:</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span><strong>Governance</strong> – the governance processes/controls/procedures used to MONITOR and MANAGE sustainability-related risks and opportunities</span>
          <span><strong>Strategy</strong> – the approach to managing sustainability-related risks/opportunities, and their ACTUAL and POTENTIAL effects on the entity's business model, strategy, and financial position</span>
          <span><strong>Risk management</strong> – the processes used to IDENTIFY, ASSESS, PRIORITISE, and MONITOR sustainability-related risks (and opportunities)</span>
          <span><strong>Metrics and targets</strong> – the entity's PERFORMANCE in relation to sustainability-related risks/opportunities, including progress toward any targets set</span>
        </div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-top:8px;margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 1: Applying the four pillars to a supply chain risk</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A manufacturer identifies a material risk that a key raw material's future availability may be disrupted by water scarcity in its primary sourcing region.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            <strong>Governance</strong>: disclose which board committee oversees supply chain risk monitoring.<br>
            <strong>Strategy</strong>: disclose how this risk could affect production costs/continuity, and any diversification strategy adopted.<br>
            <strong>Risk management</strong>: disclose the process used to identify and monitor water scarcity risk across the supply chain.<br>
            <strong>Metrics and targets</strong>: disclose any specific water-usage-related KPIs or supplier diversification targets, and progress against them.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Materiality Under IFRS S1</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">The same materiality concept as IAS 1, applied to sustainability information</div>
        <div class="principle">Sustainability-related financial information is MATERIAL if omitting, misstating, or obscuring it could REASONABLY BE EXPECTED to influence decisions that primary users of general purpose financial reports make on the basis of those reports – an EXPLICIT, DELIBERATE alignment with the SAME materiality concept applied in IAS 1 and the Conceptual Framework.</div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 2: A materiality assessment</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A technology company with minimal physical operations assesses whether extensive water usage disclosures (typically material for manufacturing/agriculture) are material to ITS OWN business.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">Given the company's minimal water usage and negligible exposure to water-related risk, extensive water usage metrics are UNLIKELY to be material – IFRS S1's materiality principle allows (indeed requires) tailoring disclosure to what is GENUINELY decision-useful for the SPECIFIC entity, rather than mechanically reporting a fixed universal checklist regardless of relevance.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Connected Information</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Linking sustainability disclosures to the financial statements</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">IFRS S1 requires an entity to provide "CONNECTED INFORMATION" – explaining the CONNECTIONS between sustainability-related risks/opportunities and information in the general purpose FINANCIAL STATEMENTS (e.g. how a climate transition risk might affect asset impairment assumptions under IAS 36, or a provision under IAS 37) – ensuring sustainability disclosures are NOT siloed from, but genuinely INTEGRATED with, the entity's financial reporting.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Sources of Guidance – SASB & Others</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Building on, not replacing, existing frameworks</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Where an entity has NOT identified a specific IFRS Sustainability Disclosure Standard covering a particular sustainability matter, IFRS S1 directs it to consider the SASB (Sustainability Accounting Standards Board) Standards, and MAY consider other sources such as the CDSB Framework, or GRI/ESRS disclosures, as appropriate – reflecting the ISSB's approach of CONSOLIDATING and building upon PRE-EXISTING voluntary sustainability reporting frameworks rather than starting from scratch.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Location and Timing of Reporting</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Aligned with the financial statements</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">General purpose sustainability-related financial disclosures are required to be reported AS PART OF an entity's general purpose financial reporting, and at the SAME TIME as the related financial statements – ensuring users receive an INTEGRATED, timely picture rather than a separately timed sustainability report published months later.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Required disclosures under IFRS S1</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Governance processes/controls used to monitor and manage sustainability-related risks/opportunities</span>
          <span>Strategy for managing material sustainability-related risks/opportunities, and their effect on the business model</span>
          <span>Processes used to identify, assess, prioritise, and monitor sustainability-related risks/opportunities</span>
          <span>Metrics and targets used to measure/monitor performance, including progress toward targets</span>
          <span>Connected information linking sustainability disclosures to the financial statements</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments & Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where management judgement matters most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Determining which sustainability-related risks/opportunities are material to the specific entity</span>
              <span>Selecting appropriate short/medium/long-term time horizons</span>
              <span>Identifying connections between sustainability disclosures and financial statement line items</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Forward-looking estimates of the financial effect of sustainability-related risks/opportunities</span>
              <span>Metrics quantifying performance against sustainability-related targets</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--warn-flush">Because IFRS S1 is a genuinely NEW area of reporting for most entities, developing the underlying data collection systems and governance processes to support these disclosures represents a SIGNIFICANT implementation undertaking, often more challenging than the disclosure drafting itself.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>IFRS S1 vs Other Frameworks (ESRS/GRI)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison – IFRS S1 vs the EU's ESRS and the GRI Standards</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-12-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span>IFRS S1 (ISSB)</span><span>EU ESRS / GRI</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Materiality concept</span><span>SINGLE (financial) materiality – effects ON enterprise value</span><span>ESRS/GRI generally apply "DOUBLE materiality" – ALSO requiring disclosure of the entity's impact ON people/environment</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Primary user focus</span><span>Investors, lenders, creditors – mirroring IFRS financial reporting's own user base</span><span>Broader MULTI-STAKEHOLDER audience, including employees, communities, NGOs</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Structural framework</span><span>TCFD-aligned four pillars (governance, strategy, risk management, metrics/targets)</span><span>Broadly similar structure, though ESRS/GRI have their OWN distinct topical standards and reporting requirements</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Many jurisdictions and companies must navigate BOTH ISSB and EU/GRI-style frameworks SIMULTANEOUSLY – the single vs double materiality distinction is the MOST consequential conceptual difference determining what must ultimately be disclosed under each regime.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Where every IFRS S1 disclosure lands</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Disclosure item</span><span>Location</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Governance, strategy, risk management, metrics/targets</span><span><span class="badge bp">Sustainability Report</span></span><span>Part of general purpose financial reporting, alongside financial statements</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Connected information linking to financial statements</span><span><span class="badge bp">Sustainability Report</span> <span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Cross-referenced with relevant financial statement notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Financial effects reflected in recognised amounts (where material)</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span> <span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Via normal IFRS Accounting Standards, e.g. impairment (IAS 36) or provisions (IAS 37)</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IFRS S1 itself is a DISCLOSURE standard, not a recognition/measurement standard – its content sits alongside, and is explicitly connected to, the financial statements prepared under ordinary IFRS Accounting Standards.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IFRS S2</span><span>The FIRST topic-specific standard issued under IFRS S1's general framework, addressing climate-related disclosures specifically</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>IAS 1</span><span>Supplies the SAME materiality concept IFRS S1 explicitly aligns with</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IAS 36 / IAS 37</span><span>Sustainability-related risks may affect impairment assessments or provisions recognised under these standards – the subject of IFRS S1's "connected information" requirement</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Quick-reference glossary</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">TCFD</div><div class="glossary-def">The Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures, whose four-pillar recommendations (governance, strategy, risk management, metrics/targets) directly underpin IFRS S1's structure.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Single materiality</div><div class="glossary-def">A materiality concept focused only on how sustainability matters affect the REPORTING ENTITY'S value – distinct from "double materiality," which also considers the entity's impact on the world.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "TCFD", definition: "The Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures, whose four-pillar recommendations directly underpin IFRS S1's structure." },
      { term: "Single materiality", definition: "A materiality concept focused only on how sustainability matters affect the reporting entity's value." },
      { term: "Enterprise value", definition: "The value of an entity's business as a whole, the central reference point for IFRS S1's materiality assessment." },
      { term: "ISSB", definition: "The International Sustainability Standards Board, which issues IFRS Sustainability Disclosure Standards." },
      { term: "Connected information", definition: "Information explaining the connections between sustainability-related risks/opportunities and the financial statements." }
    ]} />

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.IFRSS1} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>



